### Findings

**Finding 1 — P2, `/data/seakarr/src/runner.rs:298-299` (item: `fn library_write_refusal`, doc block 291-307)**
Current text:
```
/// the download layer, and the paths that call it (the `Place` arm, used by
/// discover, manual and upgrade runs) are backstops in the same
```
Substitute:
```
/// the download layer, and the paths that call it (the `Place` arm, used by
/// discover, manual, upgrade and batch runs) are backstops in the same
```
Why it is wrong: batch mode builds a `LibraryTarget::Place` at main.rs:725 and reaches `library_write_refusal` through the Place arm (`apply_place_target`, runner.rs:927), so the caller list omits batch — and it contradicts `LibraryTarget::Place`'s own doc at runner.rs:241-242, which does name batch. (The omission predates the rename, but this diff rewrote that exact clause, so it is in scope for the sweep.)

**Finding 2 — P2, `/data/seakarr/src/config.rs:1060-1064` (item: `fn migrate_search_mode_value`, added by this change)**
Current text:
```
/// The comparison is deliberately the same one the resolver makes - the raw
/// value, `trim()`ed - so the migration can never rewrite a value the resolver
/// would accept, or leave one it would reject. Any other value is left in place
/// for the resolver to reject, which keeps typos visible instead of silently
/// coercing them into a valid mode.
```
Substitute:
```
/// The comparison is deliberately the same one the resolver makes - the raw
/// value, `trim()`ed - so the migration can never rewrite a value the resolver
/// would accept, and never leaves the retired spelling behind for the resolver
/// to reject. Any other value is left in place for the resolver to reject,
/// which keeps typos visible instead of silently coercing them into a valid
/// mode.
///
/// Returns `true` when the value was rewritten.
```
Why it is wrong: as written, "can never ... leave one it would reject" is a universal claim that the function does not satisfy — a value such as `upgrde` is left in place and *is* rejected by the resolver, exactly as the very next sentence says, so the two sentences contradict each other; the replacement states the intended guarantee (only the retired spelling is migrated) and documents the `bool` return the sibling migrators (`migrate_rename` at 975, `migrate_schedule_section` at 997) already document.

### Adjacent notes (outside the `///` surface — optional, P2 report-only)
- `/data/seakarr/src/config.rs:718` — the `//` comment heading "Migration: rename config keys that changed between versions." now also covers a *value* migration (`migrate_search_mode_value`, called at 736). Not a doc comment, so not counted as a finding.
- `/data/seakarr/src/runner.rs:1683` — the public `automatic_place_target` name (and its call site at main.rs:725) still carries the retired "automatic" vocabulary; its doc text (1679-1682) does not name a mode, so this is a naming observation, not a doc-contract mismatch, and no edit is required by this audit.

### Merge verdict: **OK with notes**
No `///` or `//!` comment in the four files still calls the mode `auto` or describes pre-rename error text; the two P2 items above are small, self-contained doc corrections the parent can apply in the same sweep. No P0/P1 findings. I made no edits (review-only). No commands were run — this subagent has no shell tool; the parent should run `cargo test` after applying fixes (and `cargo doc` if any intra-doc link is touched, though neither suggested edit affects a link).

